{{also|Duck}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dŭk}}, {{IPA|en|/dʌk/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-duck.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 duck.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌk|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|*dukken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*ducan}}, {{m|ang|*duccan|t=to duck}}; a secondary verb akin to {{cog|enm|duken}}, {{m|enm|douken|t=to duck, plunge under water, submerge}}, from {{cog|ang|*dūcan|t=to dip, dive, duck}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*dūkan}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*dūkaną|t=to dip, dive, bend down, stoop, duck}}, probably from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰewb-|t=deep, hollow}} (whence {{cog|gem-pro|*dūbaną|t=to dive}}).

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Related to {{cog|sco|dulk|t=to duck}}, {{cog|dum|ducken|t=to duck}}, {{cog|nds|ducken|t=to duck}}, {{cog|de|ducken|t=to duck}}, {{cog|da|dukke}}, {{m|da|dykke|t=to dive}}. Related also to {{cog|sco|dook}}, {{m|sco|douk|t=to bathe, drench, soak, baptise}}, {{cog|fy|dûke|t=to plunge, dive}}, {{cog|nl|duiken|t=to dive, plunge, duck}}, {{cog|nds|duken|t=to duck, dive, stoop}}, {{cog|de|tauchen|t=to dive, plunge, immerse, duck}}, {{cog|sv|dyka|t=to dive, submerge}}. {{rel-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To quickly [[lower]] the head or body, often in order to prevent it from being struck by something.
#: {{ux|en|'''Duck'''! There's a branch falling off the tree!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To quickly lower (the head or body), often in order to prevent it from being struck by something.
#* {{RQ:Swift To Dr. Delany on the Libels Written Against Him}}
#*: As some raw youth in country bred,<br>To arms by thirst of honour led,<br>When at a skirmish first he hears<br>The bullets whistling round his ears,<br>Will '''duck''' his head aside
#* '''1989''', Grant Naylor, ''Red Dwarf: Infinity Welcomes Careful Drivers''
#*: Rimmer '''ducked''' his body low into his chair, so just his head remained above the table top, and peered past the backs of the examinees in front of him, waiting for the adjudicator to make his move.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To lower (something) into water; to thrust or plunge under liquid and suddenly withdraw.
#* {{RQ:Fielding Joseph Andrews}}
#*: Adams, after '''ducking''' the squire twice or thrice, leaped out of the tub.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To go under the surface of water and immediately reappear; to plunge one's head into water or other liquid.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|Persius|2|22|{{...}}In ''Tiber'' '''ducking''' thrice, by break of day{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[bow]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|IV|iii|90|2|The Learned pate / '''Duckes''' to the Golden Foole.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[evade]] doing something.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2018-07-21| title=The strange cult of Emily Brontë and the 'hot mess' of Wuthering Heights| author={{w|Kathryn Hughes}}| journal={{w|The Guardian}}| url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2018/jul/21/emily-bronte-strange-cult-wuthering-heights-romantic-novel| passage=Victorian women choosing to '''duck''' the demands of domestic life to spend their time doing something they enjoyed is hardly a novel idea.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To lower the [[volume]] of (a sound) so that other sounds in the [[mix]] can be heard more clearly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Alexander U. Case|title=Sound FX: unlocking the creative potential of recording studio effects|page=183|passage=The music is '''ducked''' under the voice.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To enter a place for a short moment.
#: {{ux|en|I'm just going to '''duck''' into the loo for a minute, can you hold my bag?}}

