===Etymology 3===
From {{bor|en|nl|doek}}, from {{der|en|dum|doeck}}, {{m|dum|doec||linen cloth}}, from {{der|en|odt|*dōc}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*dōk}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*dōkaz||cloth, rag}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dwōg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*dwōk-}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Tuch||cloth}}, {{cog|sv|duk||cloth, canvas}}, {{cog|is|dúkur||cloth, fabric}}. {{doublet|en|doek}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|dook}}, {{l|en|doock}} {{qualifier|Scotland}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|fabric}} A tightly-woven cotton [[fabric]] used as [[sailcloth]].
#* '''1912''', [[w:Katherine Mansfield|Katherine Mansfield]], "The Woman At The Store", from ''Selected Short Stories'':
#*: He was dressed in a Jaeger vest—a pair of blue '''duck''' trousers, fastened round the waist with a plaited leather belt.
# {{lb|en|in plural}} [[trousers|Trousers]] made of such material.
#* {{RQ:West Return of the Soldier|pages=67–68|pageref=67|passage=And they would go up and find old Allington, in white '''ducks''', standing in the fringe of long grasses and cow-parsley on the other edge of the island, looking to his poultry or his rabbits.}}
#* '''1954''', {{w|Doris Lessing}}, ''A Proper Marriage'', HarperPerennial 1995, p. 74:
#*: A native servant emerged, anonymous in his white '''ducks''' and red fez, to say My Player was wanted on the telephone.

